WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.030
AJA HAMMERLY: In
this Cloud Minute,

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:03.840
I'll show you how Stackdriver
Logging's exclusion filters let

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.020
you control what and
how much you log.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:14.200
From the web UI, click Logging,
and then click Resource Usage.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.420
The top of the page shows
you your current usage

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.510
and what your projected logs
usage for this month is.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:22.030
I can disable all the logs
for a specific product

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:24.630
by clicking here.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.900
I can also exclude only messages
that match a specific pattern

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.550
by clicking Create Exclusion.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.560
Once I click, I'm redirected
to the logs viewer,

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.420
and I can search for
the specific messages

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.160
I want to exclude.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.200
I want to exclude
all the Stackdriver

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.360
monitoring uptime checks.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.270
I do my search, and it shows
me the matching log messages.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.640
Once I've created a filter
that works, I put in a name

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.170
for the exclusion
filter, and then I

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.003
can decide what percentage
of these messages

00:00:48.003 --> 00:00:48.930
should be excluded.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.700
In this case, I want 100%.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.130
Now I click Create
Exclusion, and messages

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.770
that match my filter won't
appear in the log viewer.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.785
But I can export
them, if needed.

00:00:57.785 --> 00:01:00.090
I hope this helps you make
efficient use of Stackdriver

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:01.640
Logging.

